        "bodyTr": "The rejection rate for Korean visitor visas to the United States has surged by nearly 10 percentage points in a year. For the 2025 U.S. fiscal year (October 2024 to September 2025), the adjustment rejection rate for Korean nationals applying for visitor visas (B1·B2) reached 24.73%, up 9.76 percentage points from the previous year's 14.97%. This means one out of every four visa applicants failed to obtain a visa. It marks the highest rate since such statistics began being compiled in 2020. This occurred during the first year of the Trump administration's aggressive anti-immigration policies. What stands out is the disparity with other countries. Japan's rejection rate decreased from 5.76% to 5.68%, and China's dropped from 25.37% to 20.21%. The rates for the United Kingdom, Germany, and France were 20.44%, 12.22%, and 9.67%, respectively. While Korea is not the only country experiencing rising rejection rates, the magnitude of the increase is notably pronounced compared to other major nations. The number of Korean visitor visas issued also declined from 15,508 in 2024 to 12,226 last year. Korea ranks among the world's elite in passport strength, along with Japan and behind Singapore. It also ranks high in the number of countries with which it maintains visa waiver agreements. The fact that Koreans, internationally recognized as enjoying broad freedom of movement, face such an unusually high rejection rate from the United States, an ally no less, warrants attention. While the phenomenon itself is extraordinary, it is an even greater concern that the government does not know the exact cause. Amid ongoing disputes between South Korea and the United States over tariffs, security negotiations, and information-sharing controversies, there was also an incident last year in which approximately 300 Korean workers were detained at a construction site operated by a Korean company in Georgia. Whether these phenomena are related to strengthened immigration screening by the United States remains unconfirmed. A higher visa rejection rate inevitably disrupts business. Before concluding that the United States is subjecting Korean applicants to exceptionally strict screening, it is necessary to first determine whether there have been changes in screening criteria or operational methods. Only by identifying the actual reasons for rejections and understanding screening practices by country can an appropriate response strategy be developed. An alliance becomes stronger not only through security cooperation, but also when business investment and personnel dispatch proceed smoothly, and when citizens can freely conduct business, tourism, and family visits. Diplomatic authorities must identify the causes of the sharp increase in U.S. visa rejection rates and understand screening criteria, then consult with the United States to ensure our companies and citizens do not suffer disadvantages. This is not a matter to be brushed aside without understanding the underlying causes.",
